= Solution
{parent=a}

**The assertion is true.** The assumed finite <multiplicity> means that $f_0-w_1$ is not identically zero and has an isolated zero of order $k$ at $z_1$. Choose $\rho>0$ so that $\overline{D(z_1,\rho)}\subset D(0,r)$ and $f_0-w_1$ has no other zeros in this closed disc. On its boundary,
$$
\eta=\min_{|z-z_1|=\rho}|f_0(z)-w_1|>0.
$$
The joint <holomorphy> established above gives $f_\alpha\to f_0$ uniformly on that circle as $\alpha\to0$. For sufficiently small $|\alpha|$, therefore, $|f_\alpha-f_0|<\eta$. By <Rouché's theorem>, $f_\alpha-w_1$ has exactly $k$ zeros in $D(z_1,\rho)$, counted with <multiplicity>. In particular it has at least one zero in $D(0,r)$. This preserves the total local zero count, without asserting that the zeros remain together.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**The assertion is false for $k\ge2$, but true for $k=1$.** For $k\ge2$, take the jointly <holomorphic> right-hand side $A_\alpha(z,w)=kz^{k-1}+\alpha$. Its solution with zero initial value is
$$
f_\alpha(z)=z^k+\alpha z=z(z^{k-1}+\alpha).
$$
At $\alpha=0$, the value zero has <multiplicity> $k$ at the origin. For $\alpha\ne0$, however, every zero is a <simple zero>: $f_\alpha'(0)=\alpha$, and at a nonzero zero $z^{k-1}=-\alpha$,
$$
f_\alpha'(z)=kz^{k-1}+\alpha=-(k-1)\alpha\ne0.
$$
Thus there is no zero of <multiplicity> $k$ anywhere, even though the $k$ simple zeros stay close to zero for small $\alpha$. When $k=1$, part (a) gives exactly one zero counted with <multiplicity> in a small disc, so that zero is simple. Alternatively, the <holomorphic implicit function theorem> gives its <holomorphic> location as a function of $\alpha$.

